    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 14000,
      "caption": "Each level has its own vector base and syndrome registers: VBAR_ELx, ESR_ELx, ELR_ELx.",
      "narration": "On an exception the CPU saves the return address in ELR, the state in SPSR, and a cause code in ESR. It then jumps through the vector table at the VBAR of the target level.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Exception state",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "r",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "VBAR_ELx: vector base",
            "ESR_ELx: why it happened",
            "ELR_ELx: where to return",
            "SPSR_ELx: saved PSTATE"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "green"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": "ARM64 privilege is a ladder: syscalls go EL0 to EL1, hypercalls to EL2, and secure-world calls to EL3."
